-We want nothing
but pleasantries.

724
00:28:43,204 --> 00:28:44,584
-Who the hell are you, Falstaff?

725
00:28:44,619 --> 00:28:46,172
-Look, Jeeter, let's cut out
all these formalities

726
00:28:46,207 --> 00:28:48,519
and lay the chips on the table.
What's happening?

727
00:28:48,554 --> 00:28:49,866
-I love you!

728
00:28:49,900 --> 00:28:51,522
-[Laughs]

729
00:28:51,557 --> 00:28:53,559
-[Chuckles] -Huh?

730
00:28:53,593 --> 00:28:55,872
-I like this guy.
I really like him.

731
00:28:55,906 --> 00:28:58,115
-He's too square, baby,
a real square.

732
00:28:58,150 --> 00:29:00,221
-Look, you just keep trying
to put that square peg

733
00:29:00,255 --> 00:29:02,085
in a round hole,
and everything will be fine.

734
00:29:02,119 --> 00:29:04,225
-Gentlemen, peace.
-What did he mean by that?

735
00:29:04,259 --> 00:29:05,778
-Peace.

736
00:29:05,813 --> 00:29:08,505
If we're to be
one big happy family,

737
00:29:08,539 --> 00:29:10,196
we must live in tranquility.

738
00:29:10,231 --> 00:29:11,750
-I love it when
he talks this way.

739
00:29:11,784 --> 00:29:13,337
-Okay, Jeeter.

740
00:29:13,372 --> 00:29:15,374
What do you want from me?

741
00:29:15,408 --> 00:29:17,583
-Sit down, Rod. Relax.

742
00:29:17,617 --> 00:29:19,067
-[Southern accent] Sit a spell.
Take your shoes off.

743
00:29:19,102 --> 00:29:21,483
-You too, Banjo.

744
00:29:24,832 --> 00:29:26,834
-Pbbbbbbbbbbbbt!
-[Normal voice] Pbbt!

745
00:29:26,868 --> 00:29:29,077
As you might have surmised,

746
00:29:29,112 --> 00:29:32,287
my happy little group here
is from California.

747
00:29:32,322 --> 00:29:34,255
-Uh, Stanford, right?
-We're strangers

748
00:29:34,289 --> 00:29:37,672
to this Southern countryside.

749
00:29:37,706 --> 00:29:41,158
We need a guide, a chauffeur,
if you will,

750
00:29:41,193 --> 00:29:45,749
to drive us around this
countryside every so swiftly.

751
00:29:45,784 --> 00:29:47,095
-It smells.

752
00:29:47,130 --> 00:29:50,443
-Uh, you're the
stinky guy, right?

753
00:29:50,478 --> 00:29:52,963
-Let Jeeter finish his epilogue.

754
00:29:52,998 --> 00:29:55,241
-Well, it's more of a monologue,really.

755
00:29:55,276 --> 00:29:58,175
-You're right, Rod.
We're bad.

756
00:29:58,210 --> 00:30:00,143
Bad, bad people.

757
00:30:00,177 --> 00:30:02,214
-Does he mean bad-bad,
or good-bad?

758
00:30:02,248 --> 00:30:05,493
-Sinbad.-We do all sorts of evil things.

759
00:30:05,527 --> 00:30:08,116
-We're opening our own S&L.
-Real kicks.

760
00:30:08,151 --> 00:30:10,325
-Yeah. Lucky Charms.
-And Honeycomb.

761
00:30:10,360 --> 00:30:14,191
-They're very lucrative.

762
00:30:14,226 --> 00:30:17,988
-Thanks, but, uh, no thanks.
-Hey, let the man finish.

763
00:30:18,023 --> 00:30:19,645
-There's a whole number
he does at the end.

764
00:30:19,679 --> 00:30:23,097
-We need you, man.
-[Whispers] Join us.

765
00:30:23,131 --> 00:30:27,342
-We need you real bad.

766
00:30:27,377 --> 00:30:30,518
Oh, we're --
we're good on bikes,

767
00:30:30,552 --> 00:30:33,072
but, uh, cars
are a different tale.

768
00:30:33,107 --> 00:30:35,178
-Apparently they have
more wheels on them

769
00:30:35,212 --> 00:30:37,697
or something like that.
-Look, man, the newspapers

770
00:30:37,732 --> 00:30:40,355
told a story how you
got wiped out.

771
00:30:40,390 --> 00:30:42,116
-It's not out yet,
but I'm heavily connected.

772
00:30:42,150 --> 00:30:44,946
-Driving stocks for someone
else, you get peanuts.

773
00:30:44,981 --> 00:30:48,294
-Yeah, but junk bonds,
now, they're a different story.

774
00:30:48,329 --> 00:30:52,781
-Bang.
One flame-out, and that's it.

775
00:30:52,816 --> 00:30:56,889
But with us, a couple of drives,

776
00:30:56,924 --> 00:31:00,686
and it's Moneysville
for a lifetime.

777
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:03,792
-Plus good benefits, medical,
dental, 401[k].

778
00:31:03,827 --> 00:31:04,863
-Hmm.

779
00:31:04,897 --> 00:31:06,243
-Yeah, I could see you
in the future

780
00:31:06,278 --> 00:31:08,142
with at least four chains
on your right shoulder alone.

781
00:31:08,176 --> 00:31:10,144
-Wow.

782
00:31:10,178 --> 00:31:11,421
-Hey, there's no smoking
in here.

783
00:31:11,455 --> 00:31:13,802
-Okay, what do you got in mind?

784
00:31:13,837 --> 00:31:15,839
-An armored car...-An "armored dillo." [armadillo]

785
00:31:15,874 --> 00:31:18,704
-...bank.
Just like the Roaring Twenties.

786
00:31:18,738 --> 00:31:20,119
-Only, uh, many years later.

787
00:31:20,154 --> 00:31:24,158
-None of that fancy James Bond
stuff, no secret-agent jazz.

788
00:31:24,192 --> 00:31:27,264
Just plain old-fashioned guts.

789
00:31:27,299 --> 00:31:30,026
You know, like Dillinger,
"Pretty Boy" Floyd.

790
00:31:30,060 --> 00:31:33,512
-Michael Milken,
Leona Helmsley, you know.

791
00:31:33,546 --> 00:31:35,272
-Now, what if I refuse?

792
00:31:35,307 --> 00:31:39,932
-Well, you get to keep the girl
as our gift to you.

793
00:31:39,967 --> 00:31:44,247
-I'll have kicks, baby, morekicks than I've ever dreamed of.

794
00:31:44,281 --> 00:31:47,940
-So, that would be about four?

795
00:31:47,975 --> 00:31:50,874
-[Sighs]

796
00:31:50,909 --> 00:31:53,428
One question --
what's with that boy?

797
00:31:53,463 --> 00:31:54,982
-Oh, he's got
a thyroid condition.

798
00:31:55,016 --> 00:31:56,293
He can't help his weight.

799
00:31:56,328 --> 00:31:57,881
-He used to be the Big Kahuna
of the surfing pack,

800
00:31:57,916 --> 00:32:00,366
and then one day
he got wiped out.

801
00:32:00,401 --> 00:32:02,817
The board caught him
in back of the head.

802
00:32:02,851 --> 00:32:04,508
He ain't never talked since.

803
00:32:04,543 --> 00:32:06,027
-Ah, the brains
of the operation.

804
00:32:06,062 --> 00:32:08,512
-We love him just the same.

805
00:32:08,547 --> 00:32:11,481
-But, uh, he can't love us back.

806
00:32:11,515 --> 00:32:13,103
Don't we, Fats?

807
00:32:13,138 --> 00:32:14,622
-Oh, shouldn't have had
that Cobb salad.

808
00:32:14,656 --> 00:32:16,969
Ohh.
-You gonna join us?

809
00:32:17,004 --> 00:32:19,799
-[Whispers] We're a fun group.
-[Whispers] Join us.

810
00:32:19,834 --> 00:32:22,457
-Sorry, friends.

811
00:32:22,492 --> 00:32:23,976
But I like these clothes,

812
00:32:24,011 --> 00:32:26,599
and I'm not about to exchange
them for a suit of stripes.

813
00:32:26,634 --> 00:32:27,807
-Oh, but at Foreman & Clark,

814
00:32:27,842 --> 00:32:29,430
you can get a second suit
for just a dollar.

815
00:32:29,464 --> 00:32:32,467
-Easy, Banjo. Easy, baby.

816
00:32:32,502 --> 00:32:34,987
-Banjo, de-tune
your G-string, baby.

817
00:32:35,022 --> 00:32:36,989
-Look, Rod...
-He hasn't been the same

818
00:32:37,024 --> 00:32:38,818
since The Nitty Gritty Dirt Bandbroke up.

819
00:32:38,853 --> 00:32:42,374
-...why all the picking
of the brains?

820
00:32:42,408 --> 00:32:43,651
-Well, I'm starting
my own company.

821
00:32:43,685 --> 00:32:44,859
-Let's just say I wanted
to find out

822
00:32:44,893 --> 00:32:47,793
if your offer was legit or not.

823
00:32:47,827 --> 00:32:49,243
-You gonna let him
get away with this?

824
00:32:49,277 --> 00:32:50,382
-Uh, you sure it wasn't Banjo

825
00:32:50,416 --> 00:32:51,693
that got the board
in the back of the head?

826
00:32:51,728 --> 00:32:54,213
-No. He was caught for six hoursin a transverse axle.

827
00:32:54,248 --> 00:32:57,182
-I'm a very patient man.
-But I hate bank lines.

828
00:32:57,216 --> 00:33:00,530
They bug me.

829
00:33:00,564 --> 00:33:03,360
-Well, if, uh, that's all,

830
00:33:03,395 --> 00:33:06,536
I guess I'll be going.

831
00:33:06,570 --> 00:33:09,642
-Ahem! Well, I had a great time.

832
00:33:09,677 --> 00:33:11,817
You'll have to come
to my shotgun shack sometime.

833
00:33:11,851 --> 00:33:13,715
Take care of your head
injuries, guys.

834
00:33:13,750 --> 00:33:16,546
-Let's exchange cards.
Contact my people.

835
00:33:16,580 --> 00:33:18,686
-Banjo, baby!

836
00:33:18,720 --> 00:33:20,377
Where's your manners?

837
00:33:20,412 --> 00:33:24,554
If the man wants to go,
let him.

838
00:33:24,588 --> 00:33:26,107
-Please, baby...

839
00:33:26,142 --> 00:33:28,144
-I'll cut your guitar, man.
I will.

840
00:33:28,178 --> 00:33:31,285
I'll cut you a new
sound hole, man.

841
00:33:31,319 --> 00:33:33,459
-He can go.

842
00:33:33,494 --> 00:33:37,636
♪♪

843
00:33:37,670 --> 00:33:39,569
-Uh, well, uh, thank you, then.

844
00:33:39,603 --> 00:33:41,536
It's, uh, been a pleasure.

845
00:33:41,571 --> 00:33:43,642
[Chuckles nervously]

846
00:33:43,676 --> 00:33:47,784
♪♪

847
00:33:47,818 --> 00:33:50,062
-Well, let me just say I wish
your business the best,

848
00:33:50,097 --> 00:33:51,443
and thanks for all the wine

849
00:33:51,477 --> 00:33:54,480
and the use of your chick
and everything.

850
00:33:54,515 --> 00:33:56,620
♪♪

851
00:33:56,655 --> 00:33:59,968
-That's, uh --
That's the closet.

852
00:34:00,003 --> 00:34:01,556
-I hate these curtains, man!

853
00:34:01,591 --> 00:34:02,730
-Why'd you let this guy
get away?

854
00:34:02,764 --> 00:34:03,972
-You let him in our plan!

855
00:34:04,007 --> 00:34:06,803
-[Muttering]
-[Muttering]

856
00:34:06,837 --> 00:34:08,218
-I said quiet!

857
00:34:08,253 --> 00:34:11,635
-What is this,
"The McLaughlin Group"?

858
00:34:11,670 --> 00:34:17,434
-Our intentions, not our plans.
-Yeah.

859
00:34:17,469 --> 00:34:20,437
-But why'd you let him go?!
-Because he'll be back.

860
00:34:20,472 --> 00:34:22,888
-We got his keys.
-Just give him a month.

861
00:34:22,922 --> 00:34:25,787
No. Two weeks.

862
00:34:25,822 --> 00:34:29,377
When he gets hungry,
he'll be back.

863
00:34:29,412 --> 00:34:31,828
-Hungry in two weeks?
-Artsy-craftsy.

864
00:34:31,862 --> 00:34:33,657
-Ooh.
-Damn. What was I thinking?

865
00:34:33,692 --> 00:34:34,969
I'll never get
a second interview.

866
00:34:35,003 --> 00:34:36,764
I should send them
a thank-you letter, though.

867
00:34:36,798 --> 00:34:38,938
Note to myself --
never negotiate with bikers

868
00:34:38,973 --> 00:34:41,527
hopped up on goofballs.
-Hot damn.

869
00:34:41,562 --> 00:34:44,185
[Insects chirping]
-[Humming]

870
00:34:44,220 --> 00:34:45,876
[Dramatic music plays]
-It's showtime!

871
00:34:45,911 --> 00:34:48,569
-Just hold it right there.
-We've got Moog synthesizers.

872
00:34:48,603 --> 00:34:51,192
We could kill you from here.
-Put it down real slow.

873
00:34:51,227 --> 00:34:54,126
Now raise your hands.
-Now play a G augmented.

874
00:34:54,161 --> 00:34:56,197
Pat him down.
He's probably packing a capo.

875
00:34:56,232 --> 00:34:58,303
-What's going on here?
-He's clean, Lieutenant.

876
00:34:58,337 --> 00:35:01,927
-We're booking you
for impersonating Trini Lopez.

877
00:35:01,961 --> 00:35:05,793
-What's your name, kid?
-They call me Mister Tibbs!

878
00:35:05,827 --> 00:35:08,589
-Tillman. Rod Tillman.

879
00:35:08,623 --> 00:35:11,143
-What are you doing up
in that cabin?

880
00:35:11,178 --> 00:35:12,420
-Oh, networking, you know.

881
00:35:12,455 --> 00:35:13,904
-I met some characters
at a dance,

882
00:35:13,939 --> 00:35:17,667
and they asked me to come up,
so I went.

883
00:35:17,701 --> 00:35:21,602
-Why'd you leave?
-Lousy benefits.

884
00:35:21,636 --> 00:35:23,224
-We didn't see eye to eye
on a few things.

885
00:35:23,259 --> 00:35:25,053
-And if they jumped off
a bridge, would you?

886
00:35:25,088 --> 00:35:28,678
-What'd they want?

887
00:35:28,712 --> 00:35:30,162
-Do you mind
if I put my hands down?

888
00:35:30,197 --> 00:35:31,991
-No. That's fine. Put them down.

889
00:35:32,026 --> 00:35:33,614
-Now put them back up again.
[Chuckles]

890
00:35:33,648 --> 00:35:36,134
Didn't say "Simon says."
Whoo!

891
00:35:39,275 --> 00:35:44,970
-Hey, nice caboose.
-Mm-hmm!

892
00:35:45,004 --> 00:35:47,697
-So, what do you want to hear?
"Melancholy Baby"?

893
00:35:47,731 --> 00:35:49,492
"Canadian Sunset"?
-How about some coffee?

894
00:35:49,526 --> 00:35:51,666
-Yeah, that sounds good.
-Bavarian Mint?

895
00:35:51,701 --> 00:35:53,634
You can keep the mug.
-So, I'll continue.

896
00:35:53,668 --> 00:35:55,187
What did they want you for?

897
00:35:55,222 --> 00:35:57,016
-You got any of those
little finger sandwiches?

898
00:35:57,051 --> 00:35:58,086
I'm starved.

899
00:35:58,121 --> 00:35:59,709
-I should say
an ex-stockcar driver.

900
00:35:59,743 --> 00:36:02,574
And it seems that they needed
a wheel man.

901
00:36:02,608 --> 00:36:03,678
-[Chuckles]
-Hmm.

902
00:36:03,713 --> 00:36:05,611
-They're smart, real smart.

903
00:36:05,646 --> 00:36:07,061
-Yep.
-Smart.

904
00:36:07,095 --> 00:36:09,201
-They know we can spot those
motorcycles before a robbery,

905
00:36:09,236 --> 00:36:10,754
so now they're gonna
switch to a car.

906
00:36:10,789 --> 00:36:12,549
-Yeah, cars are harder to see.
-Well, if you know

907
00:36:12,584 --> 00:36:14,206
they're stealing,
why don't you arrest them?

908
00:36:14,241 --> 00:36:15,621
-Oh, we have.
-Hmm.

909
00:36:15,656 --> 00:36:19,694
-No witnesses, no evidence.-No jail, no handcuffs, nothing.

910
00:36:19,729 --> 00:36:20,868
-How come?

911
00:36:20,902 --> 00:36:23,042
-Well, as I say, uh,
they're smart.

912
00:36:23,077 --> 00:36:25,355
They get rid of their guns
after each job.

913
00:36:25,390 --> 00:36:27,288
-Oh, look, they're just
really smart, okay?

914
00:36:27,323 --> 00:36:30,843
-They certainly don't live big.

915
00:36:30,878 --> 00:36:32,535
It's almost as though they're...-Smart.

916
00:36:32,569 --> 00:36:35,227
-...robbing just for
the thrill of it.

917
00:36:35,262 --> 00:36:36,573
-Kicks.
-Hmm?

918
00:36:36,608 --> 00:36:39,231
-Only for kicks.
-What'd you say?

919
00:36:39,266 --> 00:36:41,129
-It's a cereal, man.
Really good.

920
00:36:41,164 --> 00:36:43,960
-They're only in it for kicks.

921
00:36:43,994 --> 00:36:45,962
-Do you think you could
gain their confidence?

922
00:36:45,996 --> 00:36:48,482
-Look, you're not
a real cop, are you?

923
00:36:48,516 --> 00:36:51,864
-Let's say that we didn't leave
the best of friends.

924
00:36:51,899 --> 00:36:53,797
-Did they seem, uh...
-Smart?

925
00:36:53,832 --> 00:36:55,868
-...disappointed when you
wouldn't drive for them?

926
00:36:55,903 --> 00:36:57,663
-Oh, they're really close
to the breast.

927
00:36:57,698 --> 00:36:59,734
-Yeah, I think they
needed me real bad.

928
00:36:59,769 --> 00:37:01,598
-They cried.
-Mnh.

929
00:37:01,633 --> 00:37:03,807
-Look, Rod, I got a plan.

930
00:37:03,842 --> 00:37:05,947
Now, if it works out,

931
00:37:05,982 --> 00:37:08,709
would you be willing to work
for us, work for the police?

932
00:37:08,743 --> 00:37:11,367
-[Gasps] If you'd have me.
-[Gulps]

933
00:37:11,401 --> 00:37:15,267
-It's called
Operation Weasel Snitch.

934
00:37:15,302 --> 00:37:17,165
-Maybe.

935
00:37:17,200 --> 00:37:19,167
Tell me more.

936
00:37:19,202 --> 00:37:22,895
♪♪

937
00:37:23,931 --> 00:37:27,590
-Lieutenant,
you have lovely feet.

938
00:37:27,624 --> 00:37:29,281
-It's Joe Namath!

939
00:37:29,316 --> 00:37:31,766
Uh -- Oh, no.
-Aw, no.

940
00:37:31,801 --> 00:37:36,288
It's Marcia Brady, all grown up
and back from college.

941
00:37:36,323 --> 00:37:37,531
-Rrrr!
-And she's wearing

942
00:37:37,565 --> 00:37:40,465
her man's Van Heusen.

943
00:37:40,499 --> 00:37:41,914
-Mm-hmm.

944
00:37:41,949 --> 00:37:46,609
-And he's wearing a beer.
-[Snoring]

945
00:37:46,643 --> 00:37:49,612
-Mnh. Oh, yikes.

946
00:37:49,646 --> 00:37:51,890
It looks like Banjo exploded.

947
00:37:51,924 --> 00:37:53,926
There's biker shrapnel
everywhere.

948
00:37:53,961 --> 00:37:55,031
-[Sniffing]

949
00:37:55,065 --> 00:37:57,965
-Ew.
-Whoa.

950
00:37:57,999 --> 00:38:00,692
-Uh, five minutes, Mr. Manson.

951
00:38:00,726 --> 00:38:03,039
-No.
No, Mommy, not the clown suit.

952
00:38:03,073 --> 00:38:04,454
♪♪

953
00:38:04,489 --> 00:38:08,424
Well, I'm glad to see someone's
getting a jump on the day.

954
00:38:08,458 --> 00:38:10,805
-Ah, let's take a look here.
Uh...

955
00:38:10,840 --> 00:38:12,255
hmm.

956
00:38:12,290 --> 00:38:15,085
Anaconda's up three and a half,
American Can down a point.

957
00:38:15,120 --> 00:38:16,086
Oh, look at this.

958
00:38:16,121 --> 00:38:17,467
Zack Norman as Sammy
in "Chief Zabu"?

959
00:38:17,502 --> 00:38:19,642
-Sssst!
-Hmm.

960
00:38:19,676 --> 00:38:23,611
♪♪

961
00:38:23,646 --> 00:38:26,062
-Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.
-Ahh.

962
00:38:26,096 --> 00:38:30,169
-That's better.
[Engine starts, revving]

963
00:38:30,204 --> 00:38:33,311
-Boss hog.
-Mm-hmm.

964
00:38:33,345 --> 00:38:35,761
-That's right, fatty.
Seize the day.

965
00:38:35,796 --> 00:38:39,903
-Carpe diem.

966
00:38:39,938 --> 00:38:42,181
-Buddy!
Everybody "bup"!

967
00:38:42,216 --> 00:38:44,183
-Everybody get up!
-Get up!

968
00:38:44,218 --> 00:38:46,013
Beat the day!

969
00:38:46,047 --> 00:38:47,359
Rise and shine!

970
00:38:47,394 --> 00:38:49,982
-The milkman's here.
-Duh-buh-buh!

971
00:38:50,017 --> 00:38:53,883
Early bird gets the worm.
-I'm taking dump.

972
00:38:53,917 --> 00:38:55,263
-Whee!

973
00:38:55,298 --> 00:38:58,370
-Everybody up!
Rise and shine!

974
00:38:58,405 --> 00:39:00,924
-[Panting]

975
00:39:00,959 --> 00:39:02,685
-What is it, boy, huh?
What do you see?

976
00:39:02,719 --> 00:39:04,514
-[British accent] "Legs Diamond"got an excellent review.

977
00:39:04,549 --> 00:39:06,551
-Wow.
-Um...

978
00:39:06,585 --> 00:39:08,415
-Oh.
-[Speaks gibberish]

979
00:39:08,449 --> 00:39:10,693
-"The Lockhorns'" wife
burns the dinner?

980
00:39:10,727 --> 00:39:12,419
Husband gets drunk?
I've seen it.

981
00:39:12,453 --> 00:39:15,180
-How interesting.
-Let me see here.

982
00:39:15,214 --> 00:39:17,389
It's -- Oh, I forgot.
I can't read.

983
00:39:17,424 --> 00:39:20,150
-Finally get to tear
these chickens out.

984
00:39:20,185 --> 00:39:24,810
-You just lost 10.
-Are we going to the races?

985
00:39:24,845 --> 00:39:28,262
-Ohh.
-No. We're going to Disneyland.

986
00:39:28,296 --> 00:39:30,816
-Going to the races.

987
00:39:30,851 --> 00:39:32,680
I wouldn't miss
that for anything.

988
00:39:32,715 --> 00:39:34,337
-Unless I die.

989
00:39:34,372 --> 00:39:35,407
[Tires squealing]

990
00:39:35,442 --> 00:39:37,202
-But first let's look
at some footage

991
00:39:37,236 --> 00:39:39,929
from some of the great races
of yesteryear.

992
00:39:39,963 --> 00:39:43,311
This 1951 race ---...triggered a series of spins.

993
00:39:43,346 --> 00:39:46,004
The cars attempt to weave
their way through the pack.

994
00:39:46,038 --> 00:39:47,764
Oh, one car lost a wheel

995
00:39:47,799 --> 00:39:50,353
in what appears to be
a survival of the fittest.

996
00:39:50,388 --> 00:39:52,700
-But is really not.

997
00:39:52,735 --> 00:39:54,806
Whoa!
-Hey, the wheel's winning!

998
00:39:54,840 --> 00:39:57,084
Get back on the track!

999
00:39:57,118 --> 00:39:59,362
-Where are they going?
-Car number 1 is over the wall.

1000
00:39:59,397 --> 00:40:01,709
That's Buck Shevrinton
over the east wall.

1001
00:40:01,744 --> 00:40:04,263
-But it's far too expensive
to show us.

1002
00:40:04,298 --> 00:40:06,852
-Mm-hmm.

1003
00:40:06,887 --> 00:40:09,959
-Uh-huh.

1004
00:40:09,993 --> 00:40:11,512
-Looks like Buck is pinned
in his car.

1005
00:40:11,547 --> 00:40:14,584
It appears it will take a torch
to free Buck Shevrinton.

1006
00:40:14,619 --> 00:40:16,517
-Or a really good, uh,
pair of scissors

1007
00:40:16,552 --> 00:40:18,070
or maybe one of those hog-head
droppers

1008
00:40:18,105 --> 00:40:20,314
they use in the, uh,
meat-packing industry.

1009
00:40:20,348 --> 00:40:22,696
-Boy, that announcer's
really smart.

1010
00:40:22,730 --> 00:40:27,252
-Ugh. A few nose doughnuts.
[Chuckles]

1011
00:40:27,286 --> 00:40:32,775
-Hey, what are you looking at,
jerk? Huh?

1012
00:40:32,809 --> 00:40:35,605
Kids are just too damn smart
these days.

1013
00:40:35,640 --> 00:40:39,264
Meh.

1014
00:40:39,298 --> 00:40:41,818
-Hi, Rod!
Remember, you're undercover!

1015
00:40:41,853 --> 00:40:43,475
Don't talk to anybody!
-Shh.

1016
00:40:43,510 --> 00:40:45,891
-Everything okay?-Everything's great, Lieutenant.

1017
00:40:45,926 --> 00:40:49,101
-Don't call me that.
-Good. Rod's been briefed.

1018
00:40:49,136 --> 00:40:51,552
-And we pantsed him.
-Make it look good, Dutch.

1019
00:40:51,587 --> 00:40:53,174
-Will do, Lieutenant.
-Good luck.

1020
00:40:53,209 --> 00:40:55,418
-Forget I ever met you.
-[Whispers] I love you.

1021
00:40:55,453 --> 00:40:58,628
-Nice caboose there, huh?

1022
00:40:58,663 --> 00:41:01,355
Oh, he's smart, too.
-I don't like this place.

1023
00:41:01,389 --> 00:41:03,737
Gives me the willies.
Hey, look, it's Mount Rushmore.

1024
00:41:03,771 --> 00:41:06,222
-Hmm. Mount Trashmore.

1025
00:41:06,256 --> 00:41:09,259
[Engines revving]

1026
00:41:09,294 --> 00:41:10,364
[Humming]

1027
00:41:10,398 --> 00:41:11,814
-After a brief retirement,

1028
00:41:11,848 --> 00:41:15,438
Rod Tillman returns here to the
speedway in a new number 57.

1029
00:41:15,473 --> 00:41:20,754
-"Old Blue Eyes" is back.
-...regain that old form.

1030
00:41:20,788 --> 00:41:22,031
Any racing fans will remember

1031
00:41:22,065 --> 00:41:24,654
how Rod narrowly escaped
death...

1032
00:41:24,689 --> 00:41:26,345
-Gimme that.
-Oh, that's not nice.

1033
00:41:26,380 --> 00:41:28,520
-...crashed and burned just
a few months ago.

1034
00:41:28,555 --> 00:41:30,660
-Hey, don't say hi. Geez.

1035
00:41:30,695 --> 00:41:33,352
-Well, the driver is missing,
or he's gone.

1036
00:41:33,387 --> 00:41:36,183
♪♪

1037
00:41:36,217 --> 00:41:38,392
-Bubblehead.

1038
00:41:38,426 --> 00:41:40,843
-Hey, two Nobel Prize winners
and a Pulitzer.

1039
00:41:40,877 --> 00:41:42,016
What do you think?
-The cars are now ready

1040
00:41:42,051 --> 00:41:44,329
for the start of this
100-mile main event.

1041
00:41:44,363 --> 00:41:46,573
-Hey, wait until he says, "Go"!

1042
00:41:46,607 --> 00:41:48,575
-Boy.

1043
00:41:48,609 --> 00:41:49,783
[Engines revving]

1044
00:41:49,817 --> 00:41:51,647
-Pbbt!
-Vrrr!

1045
00:41:51,681 --> 00:41:53,925
-Well, the starter is ready,
and off they go now

1046
00:41:53,959 --> 00:41:56,168
on that warm-up lap,
getting into place.

1047
00:41:56,203 --> 00:42:00,587
-[Mimics engine revving]
-[Mimics engine revving]

1048
00:42:00,621 --> 00:42:02,381
-And off they go!

1049
00:42:02,416 --> 00:42:04,452
All speed records should
be shattered today.

1050
00:42:04,487 --> 00:42:05,661
-Along with a couple of knees,

1051
00:42:05,695 --> 00:42:07,835
some femurs,
and some skulls, too.

1052
00:42:07,870 --> 00:42:10,113
-Mm-hmm.

1053
00:42:10,148 --> 00:42:11,632
[Engines revving]

1054
00:42:11,667 --> 00:42:14,635
-[Mimics engine revving]
-[Mimics engine revving]

1055
00:42:14,670 --> 00:42:18,570
[Tires screech]
-Whoa!

1056
00:42:18,605 --> 00:42:20,158
-Hey, you got a little thing
on your...

1057
00:42:20,192 --> 00:42:22,712
-Right there. Yeah.
-Well, that's about it, yeah.

1058
00:42:22,747 --> 00:42:24,196
-Whoa! What happened?

1059
00:42:24,231 --> 00:42:26,820
-Kind of looks like they're
shooting tungsten out there.

1060
00:42:26,854 --> 00:42:29,443
-Uh-huh.

1061
00:42:29,477 --> 00:42:32,688
-It's the green cam.

1062
00:42:32,722 --> 00:42:36,139
[Engines revving]

1063
00:42:36,174 --> 00:42:37,900
-For those of you
watching at home,

1064
00:42:37,934 --> 00:42:40,212
we have no idea
what the hell is going on!

1065
00:42:40,247 --> 00:42:44,941
[Engines revving]
-[Mimics engine revving]

1066
00:42:44,976 --> 00:42:47,668
Ah, it's an average day
out here on the A1A,

1067
00:42:47,703 --> 00:42:50,050
going up the coast
to Cocoa Beach.

1068
00:42:50,084 --> 00:42:51,361
[Tires screech]

1069
00:42:51,396 --> 00:42:55,745
[Engines revving]

1070
00:42:55,780 --> 00:42:58,058
-[Mimics engine revving]
-[Mimics engine revving]

1071
00:42:58,092 --> 00:43:00,267
-Grazing in the grass
is a gas, baby.

1072
00:43:00,301 --> 00:43:04,305
Can you dig it?
-So, whose girl is she today?

1073
00:43:04,340 --> 00:43:07,136
Ah, looks like Banjo's.

1074
00:43:07,170 --> 00:43:09,241
-Hm.
-Car number 57, Rod Tillman,

1075
00:43:09,276 --> 00:43:11,347
is still having a rough time
out there on the track.

1076
00:43:11,381 --> 00:43:15,420
-[Mimics engine revving]
-[Mimics engine revving]

1077
00:43:15,454 --> 00:43:16,559
-Oh!
-Whoa!

1078
00:43:16,594 --> 00:43:17,629
-Hey!
-...off to the side!

1079
00:43:17,664 --> 00:43:19,286
57 seems to be out!

1080
00:43:19,320 --> 00:43:20,770
No, he's alright!
-Yowza!

1081
00:43:20,805 --> 00:43:22,772
-Yes, he's back on the track.
-Unfortunately, he's alright!

1082
00:43:22,807 --> 00:43:24,429
-...appears to be alright!

1083
00:43:24,463 --> 00:43:26,983
-Hey, did anybody see that
Tom Cruise film this summer?

1084
00:43:27,018 --> 00:43:30,435
-Uh, no.
-No.

1085
00:43:30,469 --> 00:43:32,195
-Eat it, boy! Eat it!

1086
00:43:32,230 --> 00:43:36,165
-[Chuckles]
-Well...

1087
00:43:36,199 --> 00:43:38,063
-Ahh.
-Oh!

1088
00:43:38,098 --> 00:43:39,824
-Ohh.
-Mnh.

1089
00:43:39,858 --> 00:43:42,171
-Oh, oh.
-I have the worst luck.

1090
00:43:42,205 --> 00:43:43,310
-Boy.

1091
00:43:43,344 --> 00:43:44,622
Get out of there! Come on!

1092
00:43:44,656 --> 00:43:46,209
It's gonna blow!
-Quick! Quick! Go!

1093
00:43:46,244 --> 00:43:47,383
-Come on!
-Take my hand!

1094
00:43:47,417 --> 00:43:49,350
-...out of the car.
He appears to be okay.

1095
00:43:49,385 --> 00:43:51,076
-Whoa.
-[As Dr. Zorka] How fortunate!

1096
00:43:51,111 --> 00:43:53,285
This will simplify everything!
-[Chuckles]

1097
00:43:53,320 --> 00:43:55,046
[Siren wailing]

1098
00:43:55,080 --> 00:44:00,638
-Hot car in the old Rod tonight.-[Chuckles]

1099
00:44:00,672 --> 00:44:02,536
-That was fun.
-Oh, yeah.

1100
00:44:02,570 --> 00:44:06,264
-Oh, what a day.
-I love it.

1101
00:44:06,298 --> 00:44:10,026
-Oh, yeah, crown me fool.
Crown me fool.

1102
00:44:10,061 --> 00:44:12,235
Everybody line up
and take a big, steam--

1103
00:44:12,270 --> 00:44:14,272
-[Clearing throat]

1104
00:44:14,306 --> 00:44:17,102
Uh, thank you, sir.
Merry Christmas.

1105
00:44:17,137 --> 00:44:21,003
[Engine revving]

1106
00:44:21,037 --> 00:44:23,557
So...

1107
00:44:23,591 --> 00:44:25,870
-Gee, Wally.

1108
00:44:25,904 --> 00:44:27,423
-[Clicking tongue]

1109
00:44:27,457 --> 00:44:29,701
-Mm-hmm.
-Tough luck, baby.

1110
00:44:29,736 --> 00:44:31,772
-It's Dondi!
-Yeah.

1111
00:44:31,807 --> 00:44:34,154
-Yeah. Tough luck.
[Sniffs]

1112
00:44:34,188 --> 00:44:35,845
What are you guys doing here,
anyway?

1113
00:44:35,880 --> 00:44:39,435
-We thought maybe you'd like to,uh, reconsider.

1114
00:44:39,469 --> 00:44:41,851
-Unh-unh. No way I'm gonna be
in a dumb biker film.

1115
00:44:45,406 --> 00:44:46,407
-[Coughs]

1116
00:44:46,442 --> 00:44:50,342
-Look, I've had
racing up to here.

1117
00:44:50,377 --> 00:44:51,896
I'm sick and tired
of every grease monkey

1118
00:44:51,930 --> 00:44:54,899
who can climb behind a wheel
running me over.

1119
00:44:54,933 --> 00:44:58,488
Is your, uh, offer still open?

1120
00:44:58,523 --> 00:45:02,492
-I think something
can be worked out.

1121
00:45:02,527 --> 00:45:04,253
-Fatty, get the surfboard.

1122
00:45:04,287 --> 00:45:06,462
-We'll get you on medical
and dental right away.

1123
00:45:06,496 --> 00:45:09,154
-Let's split, man.
-Okay, I'm with you.

1124
00:45:09,189 --> 00:45:10,846
-Think they're talking
about his head?

1125
00:45:10,880 --> 00:45:14,642
-Mm-hmm.

1126
00:45:14,677 --> 00:45:15,920
-Yeah, let's go.
-We got to scram now.

1127
00:45:15,954 --> 00:45:18,025
-Yeah. Give a hoot.
Don't pollute.

1128
00:45:18,060 --> 00:45:20,096
-Can't watch that.
-Boy, they're smart.

1129
00:45:20,131 --> 00:45:25,308
-Looks like they fell for it.

1130
00:45:25,343 --> 00:45:27,138
-For Rod's sake, I hope so.

1131
00:45:27,172 --> 00:45:31,590
♪♪

1132
00:45:33,800 --> 00:45:36,285
-We'll be right back after
this important message.

1133
00:45:36,319 --> 00:45:38,149
Let's go.

1134
00:45:38,183 --> 00:45:40,634
-Get the box. You ready? Cue.
-Hey, kids!

1135
00:45:40,668 --> 00:45:42,878
-Yee-hoo.
-Whoo.

1136
00:45:42,912 --> 00:45:44,603
-Two, one, two, three.

1137
00:45:44,638 --> 00:45:47,158
-♪ We're wild rebels

1138
00:45:47,192 --> 00:45:49,056
♪ Crunchy, fruity rebels

1139
00:45:49,091 --> 00:45:52,266
♪ Pouring milk on them
is like shooting off a gun ♪

1140
00:45:52,301 --> 00:45:54,130
-It's Wild Rebels cereal,
the nutritious cereal

1141
00:45:54,165 --> 00:45:56,719
that's like getting hit
in the back of the head
with a surfboard of flavor!

1142
00:45:56,754 --> 00:45:58,894
-Look, Marshmallow Fatties!
-Sugary Lindas!

1143
00:45:58,928 --> 00:46:01,448
-I got tangy, twangy Banjos!
-Crunchy Oat Rods!

1144
00:46:01,482 --> 00:46:04,140
-And Jeeters, too.
Die, Jeeter, die!

1145
00:46:04,175 --> 00:46:06,694
-Kids?
What are you doing in there?

1146
00:46:06,729 --> 00:46:09,180
-Having a good breakfast, Mom!

1147
00:46:09,214 --> 00:46:11,251
-Pour on the milk!

1148
00:46:11,285 --> 00:46:13,632
One, two, one, two, three, four.

1149
00:46:13,667 --> 00:46:15,842
-♪ Wild Rebels

1150
00:46:15,876 --> 00:46:17,913
♪ Punchy, crunchy rebels

1151
00:46:17,947 --> 00:46:21,157
♪ Don't bust your teeth
on something sweet and hard ♪

1152
00:46:21,192 --> 00:46:23,366
-Wild Rebels cereal,part of this complete breakfast.

1153
00:46:23,401 --> 00:46:25,265
-Hey, there's a cheap surprise
inside!

1154
00:46:25,299 --> 00:46:27,854
-I got a gun!
-I got a sawed-off pool cue
with a leather strap!

1155
00:46:27,888 --> 00:46:29,614
-I got a chunk of hose
filled with lead shot!

1156
00:46:29,648 --> 00:46:31,789
-Alright, let's take it home!

1157
00:46:31,823 --> 00:46:34,101
-♪ They won't get soft
or squishy ♪

1158
00:46:34,136 --> 00:46:36,241
♪ Better eat 'em,
or you're a sissy ♪

1159
00:46:36,276 --> 00:46:38,554
♪ Just pound 'em down,
you stupid clown ♪

1160
00:46:38,588 --> 00:46:40,521
♪ They're wild 
-Wild Rebels cereal --

1161
00:46:40,556 --> 00:46:44,008
Just eat 'em.
-Oh! We got a movie sign!

1162
00:46:44,042 --> 00:46:51,636
♪♪

1163
00:46:51,670 --> 00:46:53,603
-Wild, fruity, kooky pebbles.

1164
00:46:53,638 --> 00:46:55,674
-Mm-hmm, nutty.
-Here.

1165
00:46:55,709 --> 00:46:57,228
-Huh.

1166
00:46:57,262 --> 00:46:59,230
Hey, they're going
to the turd museum.

1167
00:46:59,264 --> 00:47:00,921
-Wow.
World's largest.

1168
00:47:00,956 --> 00:47:03,613
-World's largest.
-Yep.

1169
00:47:03,648 --> 00:47:05,029
-Ride 'em.

1170
00:47:05,063 --> 00:47:07,341
[Engine shuts off]

1171
00:47:09,102 --> 00:47:12,864
-Remember, baby, don't signal usuntil he's beddie-bye.

1172
00:47:12,899 --> 00:47:14,452
We don't --
-Don't sweat it.
I know what to do.

1173
00:47:14,486 --> 00:47:15,902
-What, does she have
a babysitting job?

1174
00:47:15,936 --> 00:47:17,765
They gonna knock
over a preschool?

1175
00:47:17,800 --> 00:47:19,526
-[Laughs]
-Geez.

1176
00:47:19,560 --> 00:47:21,424
-He's so strong.

1177
00:47:21,459 --> 00:47:23,702
I love him.
-Ooh.

1178
00:47:23,737 --> 00:47:26,602
-Whoa. Hey, hey. Oh.

1179
00:47:28,259 --> 00:47:30,606
Hi.
Welcome to the 12th century.

1180
00:47:30,640 --> 00:47:33,022
-Is this Bruce Wayne's house?

1181
00:47:33,057 --> 00:47:34,852
-I don't know.

1182
00:47:37,337 --> 00:47:41,168
-Say, he's not bad.

1183
00:47:41,203 --> 00:47:43,101
Mmm. Oh.

1184
00:47:43,136 --> 00:47:45,759
Oh. That's me.
Oh, excuse me.

1185
00:47:47,105 --> 00:47:49,797
-Are they robbing a bait shop?
-Hmm.

1186
00:47:49,832 --> 00:47:51,661
Winchester 408,
200-grain bullet

1187
00:47:51,696 --> 00:47:53,905
with 2,400-feet-per-second
muzzle velocity

1188
00:47:53,940 --> 00:47:55,665
and an incredibly
flat trajectory.

1189
00:47:55,700 --> 00:47:58,254
Hmm. High-powered rifle.
Like. Hmm. Neat. Good.

1190
00:47:58,289 --> 00:48:01,430
-Hey, this must be where
the South bought all the
stuff for the Civil War.

1191
00:48:01,464 --> 00:48:04,709
-And the Spanish-American War
and the War of 1812 and...

1192
00:48:04,743 --> 00:48:07,677
-Oh, Paul, have you ever
robbed a bait shop?

1193
00:48:07,712 --> 00:48:10,025
-Ah, I don't think so, Dave.

1194
00:48:10,059 --> 00:48:11,750
-[Laughs]

1195
00:48:13,683 --> 00:48:16,721
-Good morning. May I help you?
-Oh, why, yes, you may.

1196
00:48:16,755 --> 00:48:19,448
We've been bothered
by prowlers lately.

1197
00:48:19,482 --> 00:48:21,277
-Mm.
-And with my husband
out of town,

1198
00:48:21,312 --> 00:48:22,589
I feel I should have a gun.
-[Chuckles]

1199
00:48:22,623 --> 00:48:24,936
Nothing like the wily charms
of an attractive woman

1200
00:48:24,971 --> 00:48:26,317
